ions to understand how the direction changes, and now I see that encountering an 8 while moving down is equivalent to a `/` as I said earlier, because it causes the ray to turn right.\n\n\n**Pinpointing the Trigger**\n\nI'm now tracing the exact coordinates that trigger direction changes, specifically focusing on the number '6.' I've reexamined several trains to confirm the pattern of '6' appearing at column 4 but causing a turn at column 3 in the train 5 input. It seems turns are triggered one column prior to the location of '6', with '8' changing the direction one row prior. I'm now testing these insights rigorously.\n\n\n**Confirming the Rules**\n\nI am now focusing on the implications of encountering '6' or '8'. I'm comparing the turns to the direction the rays come from. It seems the numbers dictate the final ray destination. In train 5, 8 is at (7, 3)! In train 2, 8 is at (7, 4)! In the test input, if it turns DOWN, it goes all the way to the edge of the grid! My current hypothesis is: 8 means UP and 6 means DOWN, at least for horizontal movements.\n\n\n**Validating the Turns**\n\nI'm now completely convinced. I've reexamined numerous scenarios where the ray is going right. It looks like my mirror analogy is consistent. My recent tests have confirmed that encountering '6' always turns the ray down and '8' turns it up, when moving right. I'm focusing on rigorously proving this conclusion across diverse cases, and my tests are confirming my hypothesis. I'm now certain that the turn direction is determined by the obstacle value.\n\n\n",
